5. Architecture-Fit Note
HotScan is a request-driven compliance gate: a TCP package arrives from the dispatcher, each folio advances through a short state machine stored in the plan tables, two external analyzers are called (one through a database exchange table, one over HTTP), and the outcome releases the payment, leaves it held for validation, or cancels it and returns the funds with a signed record. It is not a poller and not a batch. Applying the standing architecture discussion in _ORCHESTRATION.md (./_ORCHESTRATION.md) section 6, the best near-term fit is to fix the defects and make each folio step explicit, recoverable and tested before changing the structure or the hosting model:
1. Fix the defects that make the service wrong, silent or unsafe: the signing configuration that is never assigned and the returns that are swallowed while the cancellation continues (HS-FR-1 and HS-FR-2), the fail-open decisions (HS-FR-3, HS-FR-8 and HS-FR-10), the wrong argument in the cancellation call (HS-FR-23), the repository wrappers that turn failures into answers (HS-FR-24 and HS-FR-28), the latent recursion (HS-FR-25), the token in the log (HS-FR-5) and the build dependency (HS-FR-36).
2. Make processing recoverable: an explicit per-folio outcome that the dispatcher can act on (HS-FR-4, HS-FR-6 and HS-FR-9), idempotent steps that store the FCM request before the state advances (HS-FR-7 and HS-FR-27), return records that can be proved correct (HS-FR-40) and a way to stop cleanly (HS-FR-18 and HS-FR-31).
3. Build the safety net: the decision-table, return and repository tests (HS-FR-34) on simpler test doubles (HS-FR-35), a restorable package feed and a pipeline (HS-FR-36 and HS-FR-37) and the removal of the credential from the history (HS-FR-38).
4. Only then restructure: replace the 657-line method by route handlers and a decision function (HS-FR-17), consolidate the operation-type catalogue (HS-FR-16) and split the repository (HS-FR-29). The cancel-and-return sequence is a genuine multi-step process with compensating actions across up to four databases, so an explicit saga or outbox-style state machine (sign, record the return, update the control table, cancel the plan) is the one place where the workflow option of section 6 could pay off, but only after each step is idempotent and observable. The package exchange with the dispatcher is already message-like; formalizing it (a version, a per-folio result, a retryable flag) is a smaller step than moving to a queue. Nothing in this review justifies a microservice or serverless split, and a rewrite is not a by-product of this audit.
These decisions depend on facts that are not visible here:
• the number of folios per day, the latency the business expects between "dispatched" and "released", and the response times of FCM and Sentinel;
• the meaning of the NA status and the lifecycle of the exchange-table rows: duplicates, retention and unique keys (HS-FR-3 and HS-FR-27);
• the UT connector contract: https, token lifetime, field limits and who owns the credential rotation (HS-FR-5 and HS-FR-11);
• the SPEI, SPID and TEF return rules: reason codes, cut-off hours and regulatory deadlines, and who owns the legacy procedures (HS-FR-1, HS-FR-15 and HS-FR-40);
• which operation types are dispatched to HotScan (HS-FR-16);
• the hosting model (x86 or x64, number of hosts, restart policy) and who owns the shared-library release (HS-FR-36); and
• how the controller uses the service status row (HS-FR-6).
